# ──────────────────────────────────
# TulongGuro LMS — Environment Variables
# ──────────────────────────────────
# Copy this file to .env and fill in the values.

# ── Frontend (Vite) ──
# Set this to your Render.com backend URL in Vercel dashboard
# VITE_API_URL=https://tulongguro-api.onrender.com

# ── Backend (server/.env) ──
# Supabase PostgreSQL connection string via the Session Pooler (Supabase Dashboard >
# Connect > Session pooler). Use this, not the direct "db.[ref].supabase.co" host —
# the direct connection is IPv6-only and will fail with P1001 on most home/campus
# networks unless you have the IPv4 add-on.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres.[PROJECT_REF]:[PASSWORD]@aws-[N]-[REGION].pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres"

# Gemini API Key (from Google AI Studio: aistudio.google.com/apikey)
#
# One key is enough to run the app. More than one raises the daily AI-checking
# ceiling ONLY IF the keys come from different Google Cloud projects, because
# Google meters the quota per PROJECT per model — not per key. Eight keys cut
# from one project share a single budget and buy nothing; eight keys from eight
# projects (or eight Google accounts) are eight independent budgets. AI Studio
# caps how many keys one project can hold, so running into that cap means you
# are making keys the wrong way — make one key in each of several projects
# instead. The grading pool is built as credential x model, so 8 credentials
# from 8 projects x 2 grading models = 16 independent daily budgets.
#
# The server reads GEMINI_API_KEY, GOOGLE_API_KEY, and GEMINI_API_KEY1 through
# GEMINI_API_KEY9 — up to 11 credentials. Unset ones are skipped, duplicates are
# dropped, so you can start at 1 and leave the unnumbered one empty. The names
# must match EXACTLY: GEMINI_API_KEY_1 / GEMINI_KEY1 / GEMINI_API_KEY01 are
# invisible to the loader and produce no error, just a smaller pool. Confirm the
# real count in the boot log: "Gemini AI enabled - N grading bucket(s)".
#
# Paste the raw key. Surrounding quotes are stripped either way (dashboard
# fields like Render's take a raw value, .env syntax wants quotes), so the same
# value works in both places.
GEMINI_API_KEY="your-gemini-api-key-here"
# GEMINI_API_KEY1="key-from-a-second-project"
# GEMINI_API_KEY2="key-from-a-third-project"
# ... up to GEMINI_API_KEY9

# ── Which models each caller gets ──
#
# Two pools, and they are kept apart on purpose: a teacher's side conversation
# with the AI Teacher Assistant must never be able to spend budget the checking
# queue is depending on, and quota is metered per project PER MODEL.
#
# GEMINI_GRADING_MODELS is the checking rotation, in STRICT preference order —
# put the model whose vision quality you trust most first, since it decides
# whether a Grade 6 pupil's handwriting is read correctly. Every credential on
# the first model is tried before the second model is touched, so the models
# behind it are a fallback, not a share: every paper in a class gets the same
# reader for as long as that reader has budget. Consequence worth knowing — the
# first model's daily budget is spent first, so a day with more papers than it
# can cover meets the fallback partway through rather than averaging the two.
# GEMINI_GRADING_MODELS="gemini-3.6-flash,gemini-3.5-flash-lite"
#
# GEMINI_ASSIST_MODELS is the assistant's own rotation. It has one because a
# single model is one 503 away from "the assistant could not be reached" — that
# response means Google is out of capacity for THAT model, so the only thing
# that helps is asking a different one.
#
# It opens on the grading primary on purpose: the assistant is handed the
# pupil's PAPER on every turn, so it is answering vision questions about
# handwriting, which is the job that model is in the grading pool for. The
# fallbacks behind it stay outside that pool, and the assistant walks the
# credential list from the far end, so on a multi-key deployment it is not
# spending the bucket the checking queue is currently working through. On a
# SINGLE key it is — add a key from a second project, or move 3.6 to the back.
# GEMINI_ASSIST_MODELS="gemini-3.6-flash,gemini-3.5-flash,gemini-3.1-flash-lite"
#
# How many buckets one assistant message may try before giving up. There is a
# teacher watching a spinner, and each try costs a rate-gate slot.
# GEMINI_ASSIST_MAX_TRIES=4

# Supabase Storage (from Supabase Dashboard > Settings > API Keys)
# Must be the service_role secret key, not anon/publishable — the backend does
# trusted server-side storage writes that need to bypass RLS.
SUPABASE_URL="https://[PROJECT_REF].supabase.co"
SUPABASE_KEY="your-supabase-service-role-key-here"
# Optional — defaults to "uploads". Must be a PUBLIC bucket; the app stores
# public URLs in the database and <img> loads them directly.
# SUPABASE_BUCKET="uploads"

# ── Push notifications (Web Push / VAPID) ──
#
# What these switch on: a phone showing "Your grade is ready" while TulongGuro
# is closed. Without them the app behaves exactly as it did before — the bell
# still works, it just only reaches someone who already has the app open.
#
# This is self-hosted push. There is no Firebase project, no SDK, no account
# and no bill: the keypair below IS the whole setup, and the browser's own push
# service (Google's for Chrome, Mozilla's for Firefox) does the delivery. The
# payload is encrypted to keys the browser generated, so that service relays
# ciphertext it cannot read — which is why this is the right shape for a system
# holding children's grades.
#
# GENERATE YOUR OWN PAIR. Run this and paste the two lines it prints:
#   cd server && npx web-push generate-vapid-keys
#
# The public key is handed to browsers and is not a secret. The PRIVATE key
# signs every send: anyone holding it can push a notification to every device
# that ever subscribed, so treat it like AUTH_SECRET.
#
# Rotating them invalidates every existing subscription. The app detects the
# mismatch and re-subscribes on the next visit (see src/utils/push.js), but
# devices that never come back stay silent — so rotate only if the private key
# leaks.
VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
# Contact address the push services complain to about us. Must be mailto: or
# https:. Optional; defaults to the maintainer address in server/push.js.
# VAPID_SUBJECT="mailto:you@example.com"

# Server
PORT=3000
NODE_ENV=production

# Shared secret for the school-approval screen at /platform/approvals.
# TulongGuro operators only — anyone holding this can approve any school, so
# treat it like a password. RUN this and paste its OUTPUT, not the command:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
# Must be DIFFERENT from AUTH_SECRET.
# With this unset, the approval endpoints refuse every request (fail closed),
# so new schools will register and then sit pending with nobody able to let
# them in. Set it before you open registration.
PLATFORM_ADMIN_KEY=paste-a-DIFFERENT-64-character-hex-string-here

# Secret used to sign login sessions (HMAC-SHA256).
# RUN this command and paste its OUTPUT below — not the command itself:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
# It prints a 64-character hex string; that string is the value.
# Must be DIFFERENT from PLATFORM_ADMIN_KEY — reusing one secret means
# compromising either one compromises both.
# REQUIRED in production — the server refuses to start signing sessions without
# it rather than fall back to a guessable key. In development an ephemeral key
# is generated instead, so sessions end when you restart the server.
# Rotating this value signs every user out immediately; that is the only way to
# revoke a session, since tokens are stateless and carry their own expiry.
AUTH_SECRET=paste-the-64-character-hex-string-here
